# Scheduling inputs for balance.sh --queue.
#
#   build <target> <seconds>   compile cost, roughly node-independent
#   cache <target> <bytes>     layer cache the target exports
#   netmb <node>   <MB/s>      that node's upload throughput to the store
#
# Two terms, because a target's cost is not one number. The build is
# CPU work; the cache export is a transfer, and the fleet's links
# differ by a factor of 25. Scheduling on build time alone put
# triton-wheel - 6.08GB of cache, the largest of any target - on the
# only node behind WiFi, where the upload took ~28 minutes against the
# ~4 minutes it took to build. That one placement was most of a
# normal build's wall clock.
#
# Measured 2026-10-29: build times from run 20261029T204149Z (gfx1201,
# cold, cache export cleared); link speeds from a 200MB HTTP PUT to the
# store from each node; transfer bytes from the HTTP spans of run
# 20261029T230457Z (gfx1201, normal).
#
# The bytes come from a *normal* run on purpose. The first version of
# this table took them from a cold run's Zot log, which counts the
# cache export but not the image push, because perf-run passes
# --no-push. That under-counted aiter-wheel by 4.3x - 338MB against a
# real 1450MB - and the scheduler put it on the WiFi node, where its
# export alone ran 8m24s against a 2m39s build. Gaming's four targets
# then needed 12.5 minutes of upload between them and became the
# critical path of a 26m run, while snoc-strix sat idle after 55s.
#
# pytorch-wheel keeps its cold-run figure: it was a near-total cache
# hit in that run and transferred 17KB, which is what a target costs
# when it does not build, not what it costs when it does.
#
# Estimates for ordering, not promises. A stale table degrades the
# schedule, never the build. Re-measure after anything that moves the
# build materially - and re-measure netmb if the fleet is rewired,
# since that is the term that now dominates.

build pytorch-wheel   2336
build vllm-wheel      1409
build triton-wheel     988
build cupy-wheel       182
build nixl-wheel       180
build aiter-wheel       72
build lmcache-wheel     66

# STALE as of the --revision change in Dockerfile.triton. This figure
# was measured 2026-10-29, when the clone wrote 838MB of unreachable
# history into the exported layer; that is now 8.9MB, so this row
# over-states triton-wheel's export by roughly 829MB. balance.sh packs
# on build + cache/netmb, so until it is re-measured the scheduler
# keeps triton-wheel off nodes it can now afford — snoc-gaming most of
# all, where 829MB is ~224s of upload that no longer happens.
# Re-measure with `make perf-build` then `make perf-report`, taking the
# transfer bytes from the HTTP spans as the header describes.
cache triton-wheel    6695603582
cache vllm-wheel      2954089155
cache aiter-wheel     1520455242
cache lmcache-wheel    615890669
cache cupy-wheel       402995659
cache nixl-wheel       372131035
cache pytorch-wheel   5046000000

# snoc-strix hosts the store, so its "upload" is a local write and is
# capped by disk rather than any link. snoc-gaming is WSL2 over WiFi.
netmb snoc-strix      3000
netmb snoc-think        90
netmb snoc-gaming        3.7
